The choice of language has been rehashed countless times already, over
several years.  What's evident to me from all of it is that some
programmers here love arguing about programming languages.  All the
chin music about it hasn't led intelligently to a project getting
started.

Most likely, someone with programming expertise in one of the likely
candidates (C++ or C) will need to initiate the project and just get
it moving in a particular language.  And if that person chooses a less
popular language, they'll likely have to do more of the initial
programming themselves.  Programmers will join on if they feel
comfortable in it, and won't if they don't.

Discussion is important, but language choice has become an albatross.

